 ## 1 Program structure
-The B.E. Computer Science program is an eight-semester undergraduate program. A normal academic load is 18–22 units in a semester. Students are expected to complete foundational mathematics, computing core courses, electives, humanities work, and a culminating Practice School or Thesis requirement. The program is designed so that prerequisites form a progression rather than a menu; students should therefore review prerequisite chains before planning later semesters. A student may complete an approved course outside the nominal semester, but all graduation rules still apply.
+The B.E. Computer Science program is an eight-semester undergraduate program. A normal academic load is 16–23 units in a semester. Students are expected to complete foundational mathematics, computing core courses, electives, humanities work, and a culminating Practice School or Thesis requirement. The program is designed so that prerequisites form a progression rather than a menu; students should therefore review prerequisite chains before planning later semesters. A student may complete an approved course outside the nominal semester, but all graduation rules still apply.
 
 The academic year is divided into two regular semesters. Registration is the student's responsibility, but the Academic Office publishes the controlling rules and later circulars may amend this handbook. Where a later circular explicitly changes a requirement, the later effective rule applies from the population and date stated in that circular.
 
@@ -34,24 +34,22 @@
 ## 4.3 Prerequisites
 Prerequisites must be completed before registration unless a written waiver is issued by the Academic Office. Planned or currently registered courses do not satisfy a prerequisite that must already be completed.
 
-| Course | Prerequisite(s) |
-|---|---|
-| CS F211 Data Structures | CS F111 |
-| CS F301 Principles of Programming Languages | CS F211; CS F213 |
-| CS F342 Compiler Construction | CS F351 Theory of Computation |
-| CS F363 Compiler Design Lab | CS F342 Compiler Construction |
-| CS F407 Artificial Intelligence | CS F211; MATH F241 |
-| CS F415 Data Mining | CS F212; MATH F241 |
-| CS F429 Natural Language Processing | CS F407 |
-| CS F441 Selected Topics: Cloud Computing | CS F303; CS F372 |
-| CS F464 Machine Learning | MATH F241; CS F211 |
+- CS F211 Data Structures requires CS F111 Computer Programming.
+- CS F301 Principles of Programming Languages requires CS F211 Data Structures and CS F213 Object Oriented Programming.
+- CS F342 Compiler Construction requires CS F351 Theory of Computation.
+- CS F363 Compiler Design Lab requires CS F342 Compiler Construction.
+- CS F407 Artificial Intelligence requires CS F211 Data Structures and MATH F241 Probability and Statistics.
+- CS F415 Data Mining requires CS F212 Database Systems and MATH F241 Probability and Statistics.
+- CS F429 Natural Language Processing requires CS F407 Artificial Intelligence.
+- CS F441 Selected Topics: Cloud Computing requires CS F303 Computer Networks and CS F372 Operating Systems.
+- CS F464 Machine Learning requires MATH F241 Probability and Statistics and CS F211 Data Structures.
 
 Students should check both the prerequisite table and the current catalog before registration. A course being visible in registration does not itself prove prerequisite eligibility.
 
 ## 5.2 Grading and CGPA
 Letter grades are converted to grade points as follows: A = 10, A- = 9, B = 8, B- = 7, C = 6, C- = 5, D = 4, E = 2, F = 0. A passing grade for unit credit is D or higher. CGPA is the unit-weighted average of grade points over completed graded courses. An F earns no units and remains relevant to academic standing until the course is cleared according to Academic Office rules.
 
-Internal assessments and end-semester examinations contribute to the final course grade according to the announced course scheme. The September 2026 end-semester circular standardizes the current semester's end-semester examination weight at 40 percent in every course.
+Internal assessments and end-semester examinations contribute to the final course grade according to the announced course scheme. Circulars may fix the component weights for a particular semester.
 
 ## 5.4 Unit overload
 Registering above 25 units in a semester requires Associate Dean approval and a CGPA of at least 8.0. Meeting the CGPA threshold does not create an automatic entitlement to overload; the Associate Dean considers prerequisite readiness, timetable feasibility, and prior academic performance. A student without approval should plan at or below 25 units.
